1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,640
Before: tram timetable API image rebuild
after a one-line Java change re-uploads
~170MB because the fat jar is one opaque
layer.

2
00:00:08,759 --> 00:00:21,768
After: layered jar — dependencies in a
lower Docker layer, application classes on
top — and the same one-line change pushes
a few megabytes. Cold starts in the
cluster stop waiting on registry bandwidth
every commit.

3
00:00:21,887 --> 00:00:28,271
Packaging is not a footnote after Boot; it
is how the bike-adjacent tram service's
deploy loop feels.

4
00:00:28,391 --> 00:00:40,991
Boot's packaging story centers on the
executable "fat" jar: your classes plus
dependencies plus a launcher (JarLauncher)
that understands nested jars under BOOT-
INF. spring-boot-maven-plugin / Gradle
plugin builds it.

5
00:00:41,111 --> 00:00:50,663
Layered jars split that archive into named
layers (dependencies, spring-boot-loader,
snapshot-dependencies, application) so
container tools can cache stable layers.

6
00:00:50,783 --> 00:01:04,535
Same runtime model; different layout for
Docker's cache. Look at this example.
<plugin> Look at this example. FROM
eclipse-temurin:21-jre as builder Walk the
Dockerfile.

7
00:01:04,655 --> 00:01:11,063
Builder stage copies the fat jar and runs
Boot's layertools extract — producing
directories per layer.

8
00:01:11,183 --> 00:01:19,247
Final image copies dependencies first
(changes rarely), then loader, then
snapshot deps, then application (changes
every commit).

9
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:33,143
Docker caches unchanged lower layers; only
the application layer invalidates on a
one-line Java edit. ENTRYPOINT still uses
JarLauncher, which expects Boot's layout —
you are not switching to java -cp
manually.

10
00:01:33,263 --> 00:01:47,591
Order of COPY lines matters: put the most
stable layers first. Runtime launch.
JarLauncher reads BOOT-INF layout, builds
a classloader over nested dependency jars
in BOOT-INF/lib, and invokes your main.

11
00:01:47,711 --> 00:01:59,327
Layer extract does not change that model —
it only rearranges files for Docker cache.
A classic thin jar plus external libs
works too, but you own the classpath and
entrypoint.

12
00:01:59,447 --> 00:02:12,216
Fat jar wins on "java -jar works." Layered
fat jar wins on "java -jar works and
containers cache." WAR deployment to an
external Tomcat remains possible for shops
that mandate it;

13
00:02:12,335 --> 00:02:17,351
you trade embedded-server simplicity for
ops-standard application servers.

14
00:02:17,471 --> 00:02:26,903
Failure mode symptoms before layering: CI
shows image push sizes ~equal to full jar
every build; nodes pull slowly; deploys
lag commits by minutes of bandwidth.

15
00:02:27,023 --> 00:02:36,191
After enabling layers but copying the fat
jar as a single COPY app.jar in the final
image, you gain nothing — layers must be
extracted and copied separately.

16
00:02:36,311 --> 00:02:47,663
Another failure: tools that assume a plain
jar with classes at the root (jar tf
habits, some security scanners, naive
classpaths) break on nested BOOT-INF/lib
until they speak Boot's launcher.

17
00:02:47,783 --> 00:02:58,560
Symptom: ClassNotFoundException when
someone runs java -cp tram-timetable.jar
com.tram.TimetableApp instead of java -jar
/ JarLauncher. Trade-offs.

18
00:02:58,679 --> 00:03:11,807
Fat jars optimize operator simplicity and
local runs; large images and poor Docker
cache are the cost. Layered jars add
build/Dockerfile complexity and pay back
on frequent deploys with stable
dependencies.

19
00:03:11,927 --> 00:03:20,735
Native images and custom classpaths are
further optimizations with steeper
constraints — not required to fix the
170MB push problem.

20
00:03:20,855 --> 00:03:26,735
Keep the plugin's layer enablement aligned
with a Dockerfile that actually uses
extract output.

21
00:03:26,855 --> 00:03:38,903
Verify locally before trusting CI numbers:
build the layered jar, run the extract
command from the Dockerfile, and du -sh
each extracted directory. Dependencies
should dwarf application.

22
00:03:39,023 --> 00:03:50,951
If application is huge, you may be
bundling frontend assets or fat test data
into the main jar — fix that separately.
Layering helps cache; it does not shrink
what you put in the application layer.

23
00:03:51,071 --> 00:03:58,415
Misconception unique to packaging: "A Boot
fat jar is a normal zip of .class files at
the root like a plain Maven jar,

24
00:03:58,535 --> 00:04:08,615
so jar tf habits transfer unchanged."
Nested BOOT-INF/lib jars are not flat on
the launcher classpath the way a shaded
uber-jar merges classes.

25
00:04:08,735 --> 00:04:16,559
Tools that expect a plain classpath layout
break until they speak Boot's launcher.
Timetable images shrink on the wire.

26
00:04:16,679 --> 00:04:28,031
The API still has to answer GET requests
for stop times — and that means
understanding what happens after Tomcat
accepts a socket: the DispatcherServlet
pipeline that turns a URL into a
controller method.

27
00:04:28,151 --> 00:04:33,959
Packaging got the process started; request
mapping decides whether the timetable
responds.
